                <pb facs="0085" n="43" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v27/pg43"/>

                <head>18. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At the invitation of President Johnson, Prime Minister Holt returned through
                    Washington on his way home from London. Holt and Johnson met at 5:32 p.m. in the
                    Oval Office with Secretary Rusk, Assistant Secretary William Bundy, Special
                    Assistant Walt Rostow, Chief of Protocol James Symington, Secretary of the Prime
                    Ministerʼs Department Sir John Bunting, and Australian Ambassador John Keith
                    Waller. According to the Presidentʼs Daily Diary, the group reviewed Holtʼs trip
                    to London and prospects in Vietnam and Asia. Holt then joined the President and
                    Rusk for a walk around the White House flower garden. The President and Holt
                    returned to the Oval Office at 5:52 p.m. where they stayed until they left for
                    Anacostia pier at 6:52 p.m. for a trip with European Ambassadors on board the
                    Sequoia. (Johnson Library) The Department of State prepared a briefing paper for
                    the second Holt visit with suggested talking points for the President.
                    (Memorandum from William Bundy to Rusk, July 13; Department of State, Central
                    Files, POL 7 AUSTL) A copy of the joint communiqué issued at the end of the Holt
                    visit, July 14, is printed in <hi rendition="#italic">American Foreign Policy:
                        Current Documents, 1966</hi>, pages 639–640.</p>
            </div>
